{"about":"7043957a83082eac14fe82fd2fee22d8e8267cf00f35e5ac504f3b9944aae437","body":"The conjecture falsely equates preferring a better-corroborated theory for action with assuming future reliability based on past performance. Popper explicitly distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven) and the rational decision to act upon it based on critical discussion and severe testing. The preference is not an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past, but a methodological rule to choose the theory that has withstood the most rigorous attempts at falsification, acknowledging that it may still be false tomorrow.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"Mischaracterization of Popper's pragmatic preference"}